1. Why I Built This Environment

The original goal of the Guess the Character environment was not simply to build a playable guessing game or to demonstrate a particular RL algorithm.

I wanted to explore a more fundamental question:

Could I take a simple human decision making game and see how reinforcement learning could blend into it?

The game provided a relatively understandable setting for experimenting with search, information, decision making, and eventually learning.

Rather than starting with a predetermined RL solution;

I wanted to build the environment first;

develop different approaches to playing it;

and gradually explore where reinforcement learning could fit into the problem.

This ended up being less about producing a successful RL agent and more about discovering what happens when I actually try to formulate this kind of problem as RL.

2. What I Developed

Throughout the experiment, the environment grew considerably beyond its initial version.

I developed multiple ways of playing the game, including heuristic approaches based on different strategies for selecting questions and reducing the search space.

The environment also became a framework for:

* generating and working with large character spaces;

* testing different question selection strategies;

* measuring searchspace contraction;

* experimenting with entropy and information gain;

* running repeated simulations;

* comparing agent behaviour;

* introducing stochastic decision making;

* experimenting with Gymnasium environments;

* training and evaluating a DQN agent;

* examining how state, actions, rewards, and termination affect learning.

The different approaches eventually became quite capable of playing the game.

In particular, the heuristic agents can make interesting decisions and are actually fun to watch or play against.

That part of the project worked.

The more difficult question was whether the RL component was actually learning the kind of intelligence I had originally imagined.

3. What I Originally Wanted From RL

My original idea was for the RL agent to learn how to play the game.

Ideally, I wanted it to encounter different situations, understand which questions were useful, and develop a strategy that could generalize to new character configurations.

The interesting part, to me, was not simply reaching the correct character.

It was the possibility of seeing an agent develop its own decisionvmaking behaviour.

That is ultimately what I was trying to explore when bringing RL into the environment.

4. What Actually Happened

After experimenting with the trained agent and looking beyond the training metrics;

I became increasingly unconvinced that this was what had happened.

The RL component was not developing the kind of general intelligence I had been hoping to observe.

Instead, it appeared to have learned a much narrower behaviour.

By repeatedly playing the environment, the model had effectively encountered the available questions many times and learned their relative usefulness.

Its behaviour increasingly looked like memorizing an ordering of questions by importance and selecting from that learned ordering;

rather than reasoning about the current character space and discovering an appropriate strategy.

This distinction became especially important when I thought about generalization.

The agent could behave well under the conditions it had experienced, but I could not point to evidence that it had developed a genuinely transferable understanding of the game.

It wasn't learning something like:

“Given the current structure of the remaining search space, this question is useful.”

It was much closer to:

“Questions like these have tended to work well, so choose them in this order.”

And that is not the idea I had in mind when I started experimenting with RL.

5. Why I Didn't Simply Keep Pushing It

At this point, I could have continued adding complexity until I got behaviour that looked more impressive.

But that would have missed the point of the experiment.

The challenge became finding a way to incorporate RL without destroying the core of the game or giving the agent information that would make the problem trivial.

Once I started constraining the environment to preserve the original game mechanics;

the possibilities for what the RL component could meaningfully learn became much narrower.

So I reached a point where I had to accept an important distinction:

There is a difference between making an RL implementation work and creating the kind of learning problem I originally wanted.

The current implementation represents what I was able to make work while preserving the core of the environment.

It is not the final answer to the question I started with.

And I think that is an important result in itself.

6. The RL Implementation Failed Gracefully

I would consider the RL part of this experiment a graceful failure.

The agent did not develop the generalizable intelligence I was looking for.

But discovering that required actually building the environment, defining the state and action space, training the model, observing its behaviour, changing the formulation, and repeatedly testing the result.

If I had only studied RL theoretically;

I might have understood what states, actions, rewards, and policies are without appreciating how difficult it is to decide what they should represent for a particular problem.

This experiment forced those decisions to become concrete.

And every time an implementation behaved differently from what I expected, I had to question my assumptions.

7. What This Environment Actually Taught Me

Looking back, the most valuable outcome wasn't the final DQN.

It was everything I learned while trying to make the problem work.

Environment design

I learned that an RL environment isn't just a wrapper around a game.

The state, action space, reward, termination conditions, and information available to the agent all determine what the learning problem actually is.

Heuristics

Building non RL agents first gave me a way to understand the problem before trying to make a model learn it.

It also showed me that relatively simple heuristics can perform surprisingly well when the problem structure is understood.

Entropy and information

The original intuition that the agent should ask questions that eliminate the most possibilities eventually led me into entropy and information gain.

That became one of the most useful conceptual bridges between the game and the mathematical side of decision making.

Metrics

I also learned that a single metric such as win rate doesn't tell the whole story.

Looking at things like search space contraction, number of steps, question selection, and behaviour across different conditions gave me a much better understanding of what the agents were actually doing.

Most importantly, I learned to look beyond whether the model produces a good looking result and ask why it produces it.

8. Where I Leave the Environment

For now, I don't have another major idea that I want to force into this environment.

I think that's okay.

The Guessing game environment has reached a point where the different developed agents are interesting enough to watch and, more importantly, fun to play against.

The environment also served its original purpose as an experimentation ground.

I wanted to explore how I could set up a game, develop different approaches to it, and investigate where RL could fit.

I did that.

The complete thought process, experiments, failures, changes, and discoveries are recorded throughout the documentation for this project.

So rather than presenting the final RL agent as a polished solution, I'm leaving the experiment open.

If you see something I've misunderstood, a potential fix, a better way to formulate the RL problem, or simply have a new idea that could make this environment interesting again, I'd genuinely like to hear it.

Final Reflection

I started this environment wondering:

“How can I bring reinforcement learning into this game?”

I finish it with a much more useful question:

“What does it actually take to create an RL problem where the agent can learn the kind of behaviour I care about?”

The answer turned out to be much more complicated than simply choosing an algorithm and training it.

The RL implementation failed gracefully.

It didn't produce the generalizable intelligence I originally imagined, and I don't want to pretend that it did.

But the experiment taught me a lot about designing environments, building heuristics, thinking about entropy and information, choosing metrics,

and most importantly,using experiments to discover where my own understanding is incomplete.

And that's exactly why I built the playground in the first place.